# Tutorial 49 — Detached Signatures

## Overview

Parmana stores signatures separately from the business artifact.

The business payload remains immutable while the signature provides cryptographic proof of authenticity and integrity.

This is known as a **detached signature**.

---

## Architecture

```text
Business Artifact
        │
        ▼
Canonical Serializer
        │
        ▼
Canonical Bytes
        │
        ▼
Digital Signature
        │
        ├──────────────► Payload
        │
        └──────────────► Signature
```

The signature is not embedded into the payload.

---

## Why Detached Signatures?

Embedding a signature inside the object being signed creates a recursive problem because the signature changes the payload.

Detached signatures avoid this problem by storing the signature separately.

Benefits include:

- immutable payloads
- independent verification
- reusable business objects
- simpler interoperability
- audit-friendly storage

---

## Expected Output

```text
==================================================
Tutorial 49 - Detached Signatures
==================================================

SIGN BYTES SHA256 = ...
SIGN PAYLOAD = ...
SIGN SIGNATURE = ...

VERIFY BYTES SHA256 = ...
VERIFY PAYLOAD = ...
VERIFY SIGNATURE = ...
VERIFY RESULT = true

Detached Signature
--------------------------------------------------

Payload
{
  ...
}

Signature : ...

✓ Detached signature verified.

Tutorial completed successfully.
```

---

## Verification Pipeline

```text
Business Artifact
        │
        ▼
Canonical JSON
        │
        ▼
SHA-256
        │
        ▼
Digital Signature
        │
        ▼
Detached Signature
        │
        ▼
Independent Verification
```

---

## Running the Example

```bash
tsx examples/tutorials/49-detached-signatures/run.ts
```

or

```bash
npm run examples
```

